{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>You shall not turn aside from the matter that they tell you, right or left.</b> Rashi explained: even if they tell you about right that it is left, etc. It appears that he derived this precisely from the fact that it did not say 'You shall not turn aside right and left from the matter,' and since it says 'right and left' after 'that they tell you,' infer from this that it means as follows: even though according to truth it is not right and left except insofar as they tell you that it is right and left. And as for the reason of this matter, the commentators were troubled and gave many different reasons, one differing from the other." "And according to truth I see no difficulty in the matter at all, for our Sages of blessed memory already said (Ḥagigah 3b): Lest a person say — these declare pure and these declare impure, these forbid and these permit, how do I learn Torah from now on? Scripture says: ״נִתְּנוּ מֵרוֹעֶה אֶחָד״ (Ecclesiastes 12:11) — they were all given by one Shepherd, etc. And it is difficult: does the question not return to its place — in the end, how do I learn Torah? Rather, the explanation of the matter is that in every matter of impurity and purity there are several lines of reasoning to declare it pure and several lines of reasoning to declare it impure, and if the Torah declared it pure, it is because the aspects showing the face of purity are more numerous than the aspects showing the face of impurity, and likewise the reverse. And the same is the case with a matter permitted and forbidden, and fit and unfit. And for this reason our Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 17a) that one is not appointed to the Sanhedrin until he knows how to purify the creeping thing from the Torah. And the reason for this matter is that if at times a temporary ruling is needed to decide against the Torah because of ״עֵת לַעֲשׂוֹת לַה׳ הֵפֵרוּ תּוֹרָתֶךָ״ (Psalms 119:126), then the sage or the prophet can join to the reasoning of 'a time to act for YHWH' those minority lines of reasoning that were already rejected because of the majority, and follow the minority — as in the ruling of Elijah on Mount Carmel. But if the sage or the prophet knows no reasoning at all toward the opposite side, then he can never declare it pure even in a temporary ruling, for he is required to purify it by joining 'a time to act for YHWH' to the minority lines of reasoning that lean toward the opposite side, and the majority of impurity would be overridden. And if a lower court rules pure on a matter that is impure according to the Torah — since there are also lines of reasoning to purify it — they rely on the majority of the lower court that agreed to purify it, against the minority lines of reasoning that it has to begin with for purifying it, and the majority of impurity is overridden, for HaShem gave power also to a lower court to generate lines of reasoning this way and that, and the lines of reasoning leaning toward the side of purity become multiplied. And therefore our Sages of blessed memory said well (Ḥagigah 3b): Make your ear like a hopper and acquire a heart to hear the words of those who declare pure and the words of those who declare impure — for according to truth every person needs all the lines of reasoning, for it is impossible for him to be of the Sanhedrin until his hand is in all the lines of reasoning. And for this reason it is fitting to accept from them even if they say about right that it is left, from the side of those lines of reasoning that lean also toward the left. And this is a very correct path." "And if you wish to say that they said this concerning judges, that a judge has nothing except what his eyes see, as it is written (II Chronicles 19:6): ״וְעִמָּכֶם בִּדְבַר מִשְׁפָּט״, and this refers to what is written above, ״בֵּין דִּין לְדִּין״ — for everything is according to what the eye of the judge sees — then the matter is self-evident, for it is in his power to judge according to his own assessment, as it is written (Zechariah 8:16): ״אֱמֶת וּמִשְׁפַּט שָׁלוֹם שִׁפְטוּ בְּשַׁעֲרֵיכֶם״. And He said 'judge' with regard to all three of them, and 'in your gates' in the sense of measure, for all three of them require intellectual measurement and assessment of judgment according to the time and the place and the matter — for sometimes one needs to alter the truth for the sake of peace and the like, and likewise peace is not good for its own sake in every place, for a gathering of wicked people is bad for them and bad for the world (Sanhedrin 71b), and likewise justice requires measurement and assessment of judgment in the manner that has been explained."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {2 "The Kli Yakar's reading of Zechariah 8:16 takes the single verb שִׁפְטוּ ('judge') as applying distributively to all three nouns — אֱמֶת (truth), מִשְׁפַּט (justice), and שָׁלוֹם (peace) — reading בְּשַׁעֲרֵיכֶם ('in your gates') as בְּשִׁעוּרֵיכֶם ('by your measurement'), a wordplay on שַׁעַר/שִׁעוּר."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:57.410121819Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}